#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064031 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064387 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b2d47c1-0f0c-5722-8778-6f2c3236401b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Rate of Change of Radius or Height Given a Rate of Change of Volume"}] :question/id #uuid "808feb74-65a8-5339-b263-f92ef91bfefb" :question/math-academy-id "e-235" :question/problem "The volume of a cone is decreasing at a rate of $\\dfrac{1}{32}\\pi \\, \\textrm{m}^3\\!/\\textrm{hr}.$ The height of the cone is always a quarter of its radius. At what rate is the radius of the base decreasing when the radius is $3 \\,\\textrm{m}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's call $h$ the height of the cone and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d t} = -\\dfrac{1}{32} \\,\\textrm{m}^3\\!/\\textrm{hr},\n$$\n\nand we need to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d t}$ when $r = 3 \\,\\textrm{m}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $r.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\pi r^2 h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $h$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $h$ in terms of $r.$\n\nWe're told that the height of the cone is a quarter of its radius. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h & =\\frac{1}{4}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\nv = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\pi r^2 \\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}r\\right) = \\dfrac{1}{12}\\pi r^3.\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating just $v$ and $r,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values and solving for $\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{32}\\pi  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi (3)^{2}(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{72}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{hr}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of the base of the cone is decreasing at a rate of $\\dfrac{1}{72} \\, \\textrm{m}/\\textrm{hr}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064032 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064388 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca9039f1-d299-55fe-8242-b3a6edcb49e6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Rate of Change of Volume Using Similar Solids"}] :question/id #uuid "6ce9d38b-08d9-5372-b501-298970a2f11f" :question/math-academy-id "e-236" :question/problem "A conical flask has a height of $20\\,\\text{cm}.$ The radius at the bottom of the flask is $10\\,\\text{cm}.$ The flask has a very narrow opening at the top, and a chemist is pouring a chemical into the flask. If the level (i.e., height) of the chemical in the flask is increasing at a rate of $2\\,\\text{cm/min}$, find the rate at which volume of the chemical is increasing when the chemical level is $4\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\n![](images/eb/eb454cec7cd4c38997ee5c8e9e29fed87f491f5f47066271613bb51e0aae5363.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, let's consider the dimensions of the flask. We call $H=20\\,\\text{cm}$ the height of the conical flask, and $R=10\\,\\text{cm}$ its radius at the bottom. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the chemical level. We call $h$ the distance from the top of the flask to the surface of the chemical, $r$ the radius of the flask at the chemical surface, and $v$ the volume of the empty space in the flask.  All of these quantities change with time. In particular, we are given that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dh}{\\textrm dt} = -2\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min},\n$$\n\nwhich is negative because the distance from the top of the flask to the chemical surface decreases as the chemist pours the chemical into the flask. \n\nWe want to find the rate at which the volume being poured into the flask is changing when the chemical level is $4\\,\\text{cm}.$ So, we wish to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm dv}{\\textrm dt}$ when the level of the chemical is $4\\,\\text{cm}.$ At this moment, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h & =H-4 \\\\  & =20-4 \\\\  & =16\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv = \\dfrac13 \\pi r^2 h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the tank and the chemical must be similar. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{10}{20} \\\\ r & =\\frac{h}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{h}{2})}^{2}h \\\\ v & =\\frac{1}{12}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{12}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {(16)}^{2}(-2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =-128\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of chemical is increasing at a rate of $128\\pi \\, \\text{cm}^3/\\text{min}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064133 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064290 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93452be9-27a3-5c5d-b4f2-11b6b1896bca" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of Fluid Level Using Similar Solids"}] :question/id #uuid "5a66344e-2092-5bd6-a4d0-5663f83866b3" :question/math-academy-id "e-7556" :question/problem "A conical tank contains water. The height of the tank is $3\\,\\text{m}$ and the radius at the top is $4\\,\\text{m}.$ If water is leaking from the bottom of the tank at a rate of $6\\pi\\,\\text{m}^3/\\text{min}$, how fast is the water level $h$ decreasing when $h=2\\,\\text{m}?$\n\n![](images/6d/6de28f823109694d40bfb02afef571c2b0d497b92efbbc738ccfa15dc7341234.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, let's consider the dimensions of the tank. We call $H=3\\,\\text{m}$ the height of the tank, and $R=4\\,\\text{m}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the water level. We call $h$ the water level's height and $r$ the radius of the cone formed by the water. Both of these quantities, and the water volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dv}{\\textrm dt} = -6\\pi\\,\\text{m}^3/\\text{min},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm dh}{\\textrm dt}$ when $h=2\\,\\text{m}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $h$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $v.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\pi r^2 h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the tank and the water must be similar. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ r & =\\frac{4h}{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{4h}{3})}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{27}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{16}{27}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{16}{9}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we plug in the known values, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6\\pi  & =\\frac{16}{9}\\pi ({2}^{2})\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -6\\pi  & =\\frac{64}{9}\\pi \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{27}{32}\\,\\text{m/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the water level is decreasing at a rate of  $\\dfrac{27}{32}\\, \\text{m/min}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064134 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064291 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ab44410-8968-52e0-983f-318416a68cb5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Rate of Change of Volume Given a Rate of Change of Radius or Height"}] :question/id #uuid "3de34c88-a367-5e9f-b8de-c46953be4655" :question/math-academy-id "e-7557" :question/problem "The height of a cone is increasing at a rate of $4 \\,\\textrm{m}/\\textrm{s}.$ The radius of the cone is always one-third of its height. At what rate is the cone's volume increasing when its height is $3 \\,\\textrm{m}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's call $h$ the height of the cone and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d h}{\\textrm d t} = 4\\,\\textrm{m}/\\textrm{s},\n$$\n\nand we need to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d t}$ when $h = 3\\,\\textrm{m}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\pi r^2 h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nWe're told that the radius of the cone is one-third of its height. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{1}{3}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\nv = \\dfrac{1}{3} \\pi \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}h\\right)^2 \\cdot h = \\dfrac{1}{27}\\pi h^3.\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating just $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{27}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{9}\\pi {h}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{9}\\pi (3)^{2}(4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523990 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064291 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ab44410-8968-52e0-983f-318416a68cb5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Rate of Change of Volume Given a Rate of Change of Radius or Height"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0abcbebd-7c08-5f95-93f9-60a749c27aaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "337.5\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}/\\text{h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523277} {:answer/id #uuid "62bf8dbe-cb88-555d-b459-07ea5c638011" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "253.25\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}/\\text{h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523455} {:answer/id #uuid "6d03114f-fac2-542f-bc4d-f828eac18aac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}/\\text{h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523476} {:answer/id #uuid "70863b33-d7a3-56cd-b7b3-5735d6e956d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "162\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}/\\text{h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523487} {:answer/id #uuid "bdd59757-1ec6-567f-a901-7ffce748ec99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "506.25\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}/\\text{h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523650}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0abcbebd-7c08-5f95-93f9-60a749c27aaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "337.5\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}/\\text{h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523277} :answer-field/id #uuid "280e8158-7d58-5a49-88aa-b2cc4ef5bccd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523808}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9c01ea5e-36e4-5dc4-b2f4-1bae38b20756" :question/math-academy-id "q-121577" :question/problem "The height of a cone is increasing at a rate of $24\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{h}.$ The radius of the cone is always $\\frac{3}{5}$ times its height. At what rate is the cone's volume increasing when its height is $6.25\\,\\text{ft}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's call $h$ the height of the cone and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=24\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{h},\n$$\n\nand we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=6.25\\,\\text{ft}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nWe're told that the radius of the cone is $\\frac{3}{5}$ times its height. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{3}{5}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{3}{5}h)}^{2}\\cdot h=\\frac{3}{25}\\pi {h}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating just $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{25}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{9}{25}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{9}{25}\\pi (6.25)^{2}(24) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =337.5\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}/\\text{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524006 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064387 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b2d47c1-0f0c-5722-8778-6f2c3236401b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Rate of Change of Radius or Height Given a Rate of Change of Volume"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "229bed8d-b5d3-58ef-a06f-be1e52de4063" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{14}\\,\\text{in}/\\text{h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523320} {:answer/id #uuid "93180942-662e-521d-95f4-59f18fd03a19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{14}\\,\\text{in}/\\text{h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523563} {:answer/id #uuid "a21f0c1b-6a7d-5506-8dce-b92d689a2837" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{14}\\,\\text{in}/\\text{h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523594} {:answer/id #uuid "b18d3479-01df-5337-bbb1-e4fa31aead16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{49}\\,\\text{in}/\\text{h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523629} {:answer/id #uuid "fceac06e-7b17-533f-bd12-c185f7837e53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}\\,\\text{in}/\\text{h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523784}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93180942-662e-521d-95f4-59f18fd03a19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{14}\\,\\text{in}/\\text{h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523563} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d2e2d58-67fb-57e4-aef4-64ca1ad57e53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523812}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "babb3260-0ebb-5378-81f6-fb356d66422e" :question/math-academy-id "q-121579" :question/problem "The volume of a cone is decreasing at a rate of $14\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{h}.$ The radius of the cone is always $7$ times its height. At what rate is the height of the cone decreasing when its height is $2\\,\\text{in}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's call $h$ the height of the cone and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-14\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{h},\n$$\n\nand we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=2\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nWe're told that the radius of the cone is $7$ times its height. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =7h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi (7h)^{2}\\cdot h=\\frac{49}{3}\\pi {h}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating just $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =49\\pi {h}^{2}(\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-14\\pi  & =49\\pi (2)^{2}(\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{14}\\,\\text{in/h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the height of the cone is decreasing at a rate of $\\frac{1}{14}\\,\\text{in}/\\text{h}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523989 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064387 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b2d47c1-0f0c-5722-8778-6f2c3236401b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Rate of Change of Radius or Height Given a Rate of Change of Volume"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c472d93-f8d7-582e-a35d-5be209a4a317" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{\\pi }\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523280} {:answer/id #uuid "0e956afd-d5af-523e-bf93-e7cb03db6e05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9\\pi }\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523285} {:answer/id #uuid "35b62330-78eb-51e4-9620-36573cea83e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523366} {:answer/id #uuid "a085d722-ceba-5426-86f9-cd0b7a044c9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3\\pi }\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523587} {:answer/id #uuid "eed0c3f6-f826-5ceb-b4f8-53fcad93f069" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3\\pi }\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523756}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a085d722-ceba-5426-86f9-cd0b7a044c9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3\\pi }\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523587} :answer-field/id #uuid "125a201c-f016-58df-b9c9-cf8171479009" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523800}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9a637ece-bf3b-5e1e-8874-577affcbf215" :question/math-academy-id "q-121586" :question/problem "![](images/80/8036801735554767a33bdee0ff96a95d7e44bc3f97bacb396ec0de6d38f4aafc.png)\n\nA cone is being filled with ice-cream at a rate of $4\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}.$ The height of the cone is always three times its base radius. At what rate is the radius of the ice cream volume increasing when the radius is $1\\,\\text{cm}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's call $h$ the height of the ice cream volume and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=4\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}$ when $r=1\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $h$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $v.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $h$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $h$ in terms of $r.$\n\nWe're told that the height is always three times its base diameter. So, we have $h=3r.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}(3r) \\\\  & =\\pi {r}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $r,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\  & =3\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =3\\pi (1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 4 & =3\\pi \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{4}{3\\pi }\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524020 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064290 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93452be9-27a3-5c5d-b4f2-11b6b1896bca" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of Fluid Level Using Similar Solids"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a7f5af7-8937-5cd1-bd31-facbaffaf5a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.87\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523437} {:answer/id #uuid "67fb0ca3-d14d-5634-a340-353b7218f4cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.31\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523468} {:answer/id #uuid "9de73f60-a26b-5029-90f2-6c89c972694e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.14\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523583} {:answer/id #uuid "c184ced2-0b13-56b9-9636-16ecaf5ff14f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.45\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523659} {:answer/id #uuid "eca9e441-c13f-5522-8094-775e21954936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.94\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523749}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eca9e441-c13f-5522-8094-775e21954936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.94\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523749} :answer-field/id #uuid "10f169f1-1b8d-5c6c-9710-963782923595" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523799}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e05d5196-22d0-5390-9d3c-413318bc7364" :question/math-academy-id "q-121673" :question/problem "![](images/cf/cf9fda11d138a5227307070d52fc0a27ce9decc35c91efbb026b89d4507f2ce7.png)\n\nA beverage is pumped into a conical dispenser at a constant rate of  $19.6\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/min}.$ The mouth of the tank is $70\\,\\text{cm}$ in diameter and the height of the tank is $40\\,\\text{cm}.$ How fast is the beverage level increasing when the volume of the beverage in the dispenser is $12.25\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}?$ Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "First, let's consider the dimensions of the tank. Let's call $H=40\\,\\text{cm}$ the height of the tank, and $R=\\frac{70}{2}=35\\,\\text{cm}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the solution level. We call $h$ the solution level's height and $r$ the radius of the cone formed by the solution. Both of these quantities, and the solution volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=19.6\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{min},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$ when $v=12.25\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $h$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $v.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the tank and the solution must be similar. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{35}{40} \\\\ r & =\\frac{7h}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{7h}{8})}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{49}{192}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{49}{192}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{49}{64}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the rate at which the level of the beverage in the dispenser is changing when $v=12.25\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$ We can compute the value of $h$ at this moment as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12.25\\pi  & =\\frac{49}{192}\\pi {h}^{3} \\\\ {h}^{3} & =48 \\\\ h & =2\\sqrt[3]{6}\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we plug in the known values into the formula for $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}19.6\\pi  & =\\frac{49}{64}\\pi (2\\sqrt[3]{6})^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 19.6\\pi  & =\\frac{49\\sqrt[3]{36}}{16}\\pi \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & ≈1.94\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the solution level is increasing at a rate of approximately $1.94\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524037 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064290 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93452be9-27a3-5c5d-b4f2-11b6b1896bca" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of Fluid Level Using Similar Solids"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c39d121-da05-557f-853d-ebcb21756f17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523312} {:answer/id #uuid "2a433a35-b2cc-5308-9b6c-9ce0311fa1f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523341} {:answer/id #uuid "551882ea-b33c-50a8-a92c-3b8c3d1d3cb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523427} {:answer/id #uuid "624b5853-f054-5cfe-9d78-f4a308d6f488" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523453} {:answer/id #uuid "7ce3d77b-948c-5d21-a770-9a08beecae0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523515}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a433a35-b2cc-5308-9b6c-9ce0311fa1f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523341} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc7dbb10-4b31-5af6-9ef7-22087cd3d0f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f35215fb-23bc-5523-aa38-ea3ed45464a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-121675" :question/problem "![](images/56/561e9e9bd41dddff575a95ed619d2ff3b91a48e0845960a6265c7c7cb1dc04fe.png)\n\nA  $50\\,\\text{cm}$ beverage dispenser is being emptied at a constant rate of  $16\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/min}.$ The mouth of the dispenser is $50\\,\\text{cm}$ in radius. How fast is the beverage level dropping when there is $576\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}$ of beverage remaining in the funnel?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's consider the dimensions of the funnel. Let's call $H=50\\,\\text{cm}$ the height of the funnel, and $R=50\\,\\text{cm}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the water level. We call $h$ the water level's height and $r$ the radius of the cone formed by the water. Both of these quantities, and the water volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-16\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{min},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$ when $v=576\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $h$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $v.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the funnel and the water must be similar. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{50}{50} \\\\ r & =h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(h)}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the rate at which the level of water in the funnel is changing when $v=576\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$ We can compute the value of $h$ at this moment as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}576\\pi  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {h}^{3} \\\\ {h}^{3} & =1\\,728 \\\\ h & =12\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we plug in the known values into the formula for $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-16\\pi  & =\\pi (12)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -16 & =144\\pi \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{9}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the beverage level is decreasing at a rate of $\\frac{1}{9}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524015 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064388 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca9039f1-d299-55fe-8242-b3a6edcb49e6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Rate of Change of Volume Using Similar Solids"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12d068b6-eebb-56d2-b871-6bcee2d6ecbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28.4\\pi \\,\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}\\text{/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523296} {:answer/id #uuid "5b0b6563-65dc-575c-a5c2-d7cfc1c7a1ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25.0\\pi \\,\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}\\text{/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523439} {:answer/id #uuid "8ef4b446-6b20-552f-a8fb-dd9ac2385b7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22.3\\pi \\,\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}\\text{/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523553} {:answer/id #uuid "ae90272c-b57b-51e7-a3c4-4d79de9d2898" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27.6\\pi \\,\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}\\text{/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523621} {:answer/id #uuid "b8b705f5-d3b1-5098-b622-d4d8cebc73ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20.5\\pi \\,\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}\\text{/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523642}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12d068b6-eebb-56d2-b871-6bcee2d6ecbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28.4\\pi \\,\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}\\text{/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523296} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce066dfd-c88a-512f-bf71-f1e9a3147622" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523892}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d2f18815-e582-5459-b43c-b02a5b4a0da8" :question/math-academy-id "q-121683" :question/problem "![](images/f9/f9998b94f05eb6345d375f35f4abc7f88edfc223c4a1af5e7bbc66c4b2a2f0bc.png)\n\nA conical paper cup is $15\\,\\text{m}$ tall with a diameter of $16\\,\\text{m}.$ The cup is being filled with water so that the water level rises at a rate of $1\\,\\text{m/h}.$ At what rate is the water volume increasing when the water level is $10\\,\\text{m}?$ Round your answer to one decimal place." :question/worked-solution "First, let's consider the dimensions of the cup. Let's call $H=15\\,\\text{m}$ the height of the conical cup, and $R=\\frac{16}{2}=8\\,\\text{m}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the water level. We call $h$ the height of the water and $r$ the radius of the cone formed by the water. Both of these quantities, and the water volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=1\\,\\text{m}/\\text{h},\n$$\n\nand we want to know $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=10\\,\\text{m}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the cup and the water must be similar. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{8}{15} \\\\ r & =\\frac{8}{15}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{8}{15}h)}^{2}h \\\\ v & =\\frac{64}{675}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{64}{675}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{64}{225}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the rate at which the volume of water in the cup is changing when $h=10\\,\\text{m}.$ \nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{64}{225}\\pi (10)^{2}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & ≈28.4\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/\\text{h}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.\n\nTherefore, the water volume increases at a rate of $28.4\\pi \\,\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}\\text{/h}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523929 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064388 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca9039f1-d299-55fe-8242-b3a6edcb49e6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Rate of Change of Volume Using Similar Solids"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d7a46df-5161-5d18-a42e-a47e5c2a3b8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "56.9\\pi \\,\\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523518} {:answer/id #uuid "ab19582b-2dba-57b9-a8c1-04ac521939f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65.2\\pi \\,\\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523613} {:answer/id #uuid "d6bc13e0-926a-5c5a-9aad-2a074883b5aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32.6\\pi \\,\\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523706} {:answer/id #uuid "da026eb5-0310-5dc3-beb3-c16b05bbfd8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "44.8\\pi \\,\\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523709} {:answer/id #uuid "f4822ccd-132b-598c-bd55-642e2fc02eba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "69.5\\pi \\,\\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523764}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d7a46df-5161-5d18-a42e-a47e5c2a3b8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "56.9\\pi \\,\\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523518} :answer-field/id #uuid "591430e3-f96f-5cc8-bf64-cc62f5a99def" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523833}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0c589335-1cfb-54d8-9a5c-d454270a6ef5" :question/math-academy-id "q-121687" :question/problem "![](images/d3/d330e7c959ede9f969573752b9f67e3ce73c732a680b58cd2f9f466f1cf5db4c.png)\n\nA conical container is $21\\,\\text{ft}$ tall with a diameter of $14\\,\\text{ft}.$ The container is being emptied so that the liquid level lowers at a rate of $2\\,\\text{ft/min}.$ At what rate is the liquid volume decreasing when the liquid level is $16\\,\\text{ft}?$ Round your answer to one decimal place." :question/worked-solution "First, let's consider the dimensions of the container. Let's call $H=21\\,\\text{ft}$ the height of the conical container, and $R=\\frac{14}{2}=7\\,\\text{ft}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the liquid level. We call $h$ the height of the liquid and $r$ the radius of the cone formed by the liquid. Both of these quantities, and the liquid volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-2\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{min},\n$$\n\nand we want to know $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=16\\,\\text{ft}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the container and the liquid must be similar. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{7}{21} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{3}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{1}{3}h)}^{2}h \\\\ v & =\\frac{1}{27}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{27}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{9}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the rate at which the volume of water in the cup is changing when $h=16\\,\\text{ft}.$ \nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{9}\\pi (16)^{2}(-2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & ≈-56.9\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}/\\text{min}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.\n\nTherefore, the liquid volume decreases at a rate of approximately $56.9\\pi \\,\\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}\\text{/min}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064623 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064291 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ab44410-8968-52e0-983f-318416a68cb5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Rate of Change of Volume Given a Rate of Change of Radius or Height"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0021e499-c9c9-5399-ab15-a808d440e793" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "180\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523249} {:answer/id #uuid "333c09d3-9ab7-5199-a6ee-8a0523c90ba3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "135\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523362} {:answer/id #uuid "57891982-57da-5fc3-b173-c9da485953c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "405\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523433} {:answer/id #uuid "a2ab9bda-f54f-5fd3-9a70-40b55b3c8caf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523598} {:answer/id #uuid "d7ee1130-7728-5761-bf36-0adeb71dec78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523708}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57891982-57da-5fc3-b173-c9da485953c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "405\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523433} :answer-field/id #uuid "06ff5732-0542-503e-ad44-0dfc812e1368" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061800}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "80257adf-7e8c-5147-9ca1-4f36f76a17ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-23069" :question/problem "The height of a cone is increasing at a rate of $5\\,\\text{in}/\\text{min}.$ The diameter of the cone is always $3$ times its height. At what rate is the cone's volume increasing when its height is $6\\,\\text{in}?$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let's call $h$ the height of the cone and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=5\\,\\text{in}\\text{/min},\n$$\n\nand we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=6\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nWe're told that the diameter is $3$ times the height. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =3h \\\\ 2r & =3h \\\\ r & =\\frac{3}{2}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{3}{2}h)}^{2}\\cdot h=\\frac{3}{4}\\pi {h}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating just $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{4}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{9}{4}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{9}{4}\\pi (6)^{2}(5) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =405\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523977 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064290 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93452be9-27a3-5c5d-b4f2-11b6b1896bca" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of Fluid Level Using Similar Solids"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00e67d9a-a462-5f7e-b2cd-0ba68c332cb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,\\text{cm/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523251} {:answer/id #uuid "468e364f-adb7-5316-81c4-30d5f46d5a70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\,\\text{cm}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523399} {:answer/id #uuid "c7163626-4b88-545e-8c81-82995ff45ea0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi \\,\\text{cm}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523672} {:answer/id #uuid "d10f03ec-49e1-57de-bfcd-f75d2f7decf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523693} {:answer/id #uuid "d6781a0a-396a-5cdc-9fb4-752741391dc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523705}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00e67d9a-a462-5f7e-b2cd-0ba68c332cb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,\\text{cm/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523251} :answer-field/id #uuid "0403bdc2-182d-5f43-a2bb-37b77dc8facc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523792}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "829ceea0-b4f0-5bef-b797-d4b161d8e247" :question/math-academy-id "q-2558" :question/problem "![](images/a9/a9c0d78ce05859db77dcc1e5198596a1a02c798c607ba9c4ff78e6fe1362b113.png)\n\nThe height of a conical cup is $6\\text{ cm}$ and the opening radius is $2\\text{ cm}.$ Water leaks out of the bottom at the rate of $2\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}.$ If the water level is $3\\,\\text{cm}$ at a particular moment, how fast is the water level decreasing at that moment?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's consider the dimensions of the cup. Let's call $H=6\\,\\text{cm}$ the height of the cup, and $R=2\\,\\text{cm}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the water level. We call $h$ the water level's height and $r$ the radius of the cone formed by the water. Both of these quantities, and the water volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-2\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=3\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $h$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $v.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the cup and the water must be similar. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{2}{6} \\\\ r & =\\frac{h}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{h}{3})}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{27}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{9}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we plug in the known values, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =\\frac{1}{9}\\pi ({3}^{2})\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -2 & =\\pi \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the water level is decreasing at a rate of $\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,\\text{cm/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523966 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064290 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93452be9-27a3-5c5d-b4f2-11b6b1896bca" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of Fluid Level Using Similar Solids"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0249d478-3dcc-5311-b2c6-8d70031c6a4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5\\pi }\\,\\text{cm/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523255} {:answer/id #uuid "119c72b2-8cca-5163-9ca8-2e6e7e6248ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{9}\\,\\text{cm/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523292} {:answer/id #uuid "27554380-74ae-59cc-9a00-1703f6d7f6b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5\\pi }\\,\\text{cm/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523333} {:answer/id #uuid "b50bac0d-ca11-5f45-b354-8530b1736b79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{9\\pi }\\,\\text{cm/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523635} {:answer/id #uuid "c45c9a72-add8-5a9c-905f-a2e2391214d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3\\pi }\\,\\text{cm/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b50bac0d-ca11-5f45-b354-8530b1736b79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{9\\pi }\\,\\text{cm/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523635} :answer-field/id #uuid "12d04c6e-e4aa-5010-b471-aea192835835" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523801}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "734a1589-9b26-5ca8-b559-8254d0411564" :question/math-academy-id "q-2840" :question/problem "![](images/77/7732722fc875e8cf13482394990baa0b5f8306a8151fca934491bb12dd181d9e.png)\n\nA  $10\\,\\text{cm}$ tall funnel containing water is being drained at a constant rate of  $10\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}.$ The mouth of the funnel is $12\\,\\text{cm}$ in diameter. How fast is the water level dropping when there is $15\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}$ of water remaining in the funnel?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's consider the dimensions of the funnel. Let's call $H=10\\,\\text{cm}$ the height of the funnel, and $R=\\frac{12}{2}=6\\,\\text{cm}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the water level. We call $h$ the water level's height and $r$ the radius of the cone formed by the water. Both of these quantities, and the water volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-10\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$ when $v=15\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $h$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $v.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the funnel and the water must be similar. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{6}{10} \\\\ r & =\\frac{3h}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{3h}{5})}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{25}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{25}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{9}{25}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the rate at which the level of water in the funnel is changing when $v=15\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$ We can compute the value of $h$ at this moment as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15\\pi  & =\\frac{3}{25}\\pi {h}^{3} \\\\ {h}^{3} & =125 \\\\ h & =5\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we plug in the known values into the formula for $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10 & =\\frac{9}{25}\\pi ({5}^{2})\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -10 & =9\\pi \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{10}{9\\pi }\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the water level is decreasing at a rate of $\\frac{10}{9\\pi }\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524000 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064388 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca9039f1-d299-55fe-8242-b3a6edcb49e6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Rate of Change of Volume Using Similar Solids"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3325c758-6521-5b3c-b952-88a1c7f3a5c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96\\pi {\\text{ in}}^{3}\\text{/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523361} {:answer/id #uuid "34f99a8a-1aff-5e48-8263-1c14bb296f09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60\\pi {\\text{ in}}^{3}\\text{/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523364} {:answer/id #uuid "5e041c4f-bd1d-58b3-a192-9214f42c132b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30\\pi {\\text{ in}}^{3}\\text{/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523445} {:answer/id #uuid "8026da22-c9eb-5c97-bc44-dd272fdbe3e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54\\pi {\\text{ in}}^{3}\\text{/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523526} {:answer/id #uuid "e72d9225-9845-59c5-9e04-07e4b8235685" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40\\pi {\\text{ in}}^{3}\\text{/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523741}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e041c4f-bd1d-58b3-a192-9214f42c132b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30\\pi {\\text{ in}}^{3}\\text{/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523445} :answer-field/id #uuid "60ec6a45-4404-5e20-a3cb-94bf7320ffb4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a9bed4a9-ed2b-5603-85e6-df6b8285a648" :question/math-academy-id "q-2858" :question/problem "![](images/51/51ee41eea6dfef1292c68a13a438f43c327639e01452e29998bbe513ac3838ba.png)\n\nA container has the shape of a right circular cone. The height of the container is $20\\,\\text{in}$ and the diameter at the top of the container is $10\\,\\text{in}.$ Water in the container is evaporating so that its depth, $h$, is decreasing at a constant rate of $\\frac{10}{3}\\,\\text{in/h}.$ At what rate is the volume of water decreasing when the water depth is $12\\,\\text{in}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's consider the dimensions of the container. Let's call $H=20\\,\\text{in}$ the height of the conical container, and $R=\\frac{10}{2}=5\\,\\text{in}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the water level. We call $h$ the height of the water and $r$ the radius of the cone formed by the water. Both of these quantities, and the water volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{10}{3}\\,\\text{in}/\\text{h},\n$$\n\nand we want to know $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=12\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the container and the water must be similar. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{5}{20} \\\\ r & =\\frac{h}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{h}{4})}^{2}h \\\\ v & =\\frac{1}{48}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{48}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{16}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the rate at which the volume of water in the container is changing when $h=12\\,\\text{in}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{16}\\pi (12)^{2}(-\\frac{10}{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =-30\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of water is decreasing at a rate of $30\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{h}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523973 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064387 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b2d47c1-0f0c-5722-8778-6f2c3236401b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Rate of Change of Radius or Height Given a Rate of Change of Volume"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0258576c-2f5d-548e-804e-389c4853daef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523256}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0258576c-2f5d-548e-804e-389c4853daef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523256} :answer-field/id #uuid "e388d934-e923-52d0-a739-f7b5c411547b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523905}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7cce7dd9-a52f-5429-96fd-d564ac81f3b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-314057" :question/problem "The volume of a cone is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{3}{4}\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}\\text{/s}.$ The diameter of the cone is always one third of its height.  Find the rate of the cone's height increase when the  height is $6\\,\\text{m}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe height is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} $\\text{ m/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's call $h$ the height of the cone and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{3}{4}\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}\\text{/s},\n$$\n\nand we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=6\\,\\text{m}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $h$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $v.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nWe're told that the diameter is one third of the height. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{1}{3}h \\\\ 2r & =\\frac{1}{3}h \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{6}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{1}{6}h)}^{2}h=\\frac{1}{108}\\pi {h}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{36}\\pi {h}^{2}(\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}).\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}\\pi  & =\\frac{1}{36}\\pi (6)^{2}(\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the cone's height is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{3}{4}\\text{ m/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523968 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064291 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ab44410-8968-52e0-983f-318416a68cb5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Rate of Change of Volume Given a Rate of Change of Radius or Height"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dab41013-31c6-5b8c-b810-4bf4de38c3b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523711}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dab41013-31c6-5b8c-b810-4bf4de38c3b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523711} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d0b2151-e0e8-5527-9aa9-647f2c498e2c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523844}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "748701f6-8780-55dc-bf9a-808d48cf28f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-344973" :question/problem "The height of a cone is increasing at a rate of $4\\,\\text{in}/\\text{s}.$ The diameter of the cone is always $4$ times its height. Find the rate of the cone's volume increase when height is $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{in}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe volume is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's call $h$ the height of the cone and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=4\\,\\text{in}\\text{/s},\n$$\n\nand we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nWe're told that the diameter is $4$ times the height. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =4h \\\\ 2r & =4h \\\\ r & =2h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(2h)}^{2}\\cdot h=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {h}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating just $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}(4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the cone's volume is increasing at a rate of $4\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523998 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064291 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ab44410-8968-52e0-983f-318416a68cb5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Rate of Change of Volume Given a Rate of Change of Radius or Height"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa70caeb-2f25-52b2-8b3f-fb7c469344cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523778}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa70caeb-2f25-52b2-8b3f-fb7c469344cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523778} :answer-field/id #uuid "feafb06a-b02a-5dd2-94bc-ae63b61d224f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523919}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a8aac6cd-427e-5b7e-aea2-44c84e419786" :question/math-academy-id "q-344979" :question/problem "The height of a cone is decreasing at a rate of $6\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.$ The diameter of the cone is always $4$ times its height. Find the rate of the cone's volume decrease when the height is $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{cm}.$ Do not round the answer. \n\n*Hint*: Assume that the rate of decrease is positive.\n\nThe volume is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's call $h$ the height of the cone and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-6\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s},\n$$\n\nand we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nWe're told that the diameter is $4$ times the height. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =4h \\\\ 2r & =4h \\\\ r & =2h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(2h)}^{2}\\cdot h=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {h}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating just $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}(-6) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =-6\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the cone's volume is decreasing at a rate of $6\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524011 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064387 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b2d47c1-0f0c-5722-8778-6f2c3236401b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Rate of Change of Radius or Height Given a Rate of Change of Volume"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11a5e07f-0354-53c6-9022-843d420732f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9\\pi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523293}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11a5e07f-0354-53c6-9022-843d420732f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9\\pi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523293} :answer-field/id #uuid "b00b4530-77ae-52d8-a12b-a7e5a3f6636e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cc3b1d02-1e54-57cd-be11-d438beee9721" :question/math-academy-id "q-345004" :question/problem "![](images/a3/a3623402e5d5df9339f1a3bb615c92ff5fcff9ae8d96c3630c5cb847a3038eda.png)\n\nWater is leaking out of a cone-shaped container so that the volume of the water decreases at a rate of $12\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}\\text{/min}.$ The height of the container is three times its base radius. Find the rate of decrease of the radius of the water's surface when the radius is $6\\,\\text{in}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n*Hint*: Assume that the rate of decrease is positive.\n\nThe radius is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} $\\text{in}/\\text{min}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's call $h$ the height of the conical volume of water and $r$ be the radius of its surface. Both of these quantities, as well as the water's volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-12\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{min},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}$ when $r=6\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we want the rate of change, which is $r.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $h$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $h$ in terms of $r.$\n\nWe're told that the height is always three times its base radius. So, we have $h=3r.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}(3r) \\\\  & =\\pi {r}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $r,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\  & =3\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-12 & =3\\pi (6)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -12 & =108\\pi \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{9\\pi }\\,\\text{in}/\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of the water's surface decreases at a rate of $\\frac{1}{9\\pi }\\,\\text{in}/\\text{min}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524023 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064388 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca9039f1-d299-55fe-8242-b3a6edcb49e6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Rate of Change of Volume Using Similar Solids"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c7c0f8d-b4b8-5c06-9ffe-1250080e6409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523313}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c7c0f8d-b4b8-5c06-9ffe-1250080e6409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523313} :answer-field/id #uuid "5cf71d78-57d0-513b-a028-613da517c54d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523835}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e4ae8f6c-f256-5ee8-80b1-72f1337deae2" :question/math-academy-id "q-346239" :question/problem "![](images/01/01d6575ff056e7f99075597547583b4c648fbe6ad462d38a5d5fd76b8c73a564.png)\n\nA conical container has a height of $8\\,\\text{cm},$ and the radius at the bottom of the container is $4\\,\\text{cm}.$ Batter is being poured into the container. If the level of the batter in the container is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\,\\text{cm/s}$, find the rate at which the volume of the batter is increasing when the radius of the batter surface is $\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{cm}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe volume of batter is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's consider the dimensions of the container. Let's call $H=8\\,\\text{cm}$ the height of the conical container, and $R=4\\,\\text{cm}$ its radius at the bottom. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the batter level. We call $h$ the distance from the top of the container to the batter surface, $r$ the radius of the cone at the surface of the batter, and $v$ the volume of the empty space in the container. All of these quantities change with time. In particular, we are given\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s},\n$$\n\nwhich is negative because the distance from the top of the container to the batter surface decreases as we pour the batter into the container.\n\nWe want to know $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $r=\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the container and the batter must be similar. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{4}{8} \\\\ r & =\\frac{h}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{h}{2})}^{2}h \\\\ v & =\\frac{1}{12}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{12}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to know $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $r=\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{cm}.$ We can compute the value of $h$ at this moment as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} & =\\frac{h}{2} \\\\ h & =3\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi (3)^{2}(-\\frac{4}{\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =-9\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of batter is increasing at a rate of $9\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523993 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064388 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca9039f1-d299-55fe-8242-b3a6edcb49e6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Rate of Change of Volume Using Similar Solids"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4ec126f-fbd1-54e2-843e-0b31946cebb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523701}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4ec126f-fbd1-54e2-843e-0b31946cebb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523701} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a63ee4c-86fd-5c07-bea8-874c24302452" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523827}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9ddf5a2d-9bc3-5a54-8df4-57b5fda24b44" :question/math-academy-id "q-346630" :question/problem "![](images/e5/e56e31d999674075f3af880e8eadf14cd978f2dba73dd946dc103aae298f6681.png)\n\nAn inverted right circular conical tank contains sand. The height of the tank is $24\\,\\text{in}$ and the diameter of its top base is $16\\,\\text{in}.$ The sand is draining from the bottom of the tank so that the height of the sand, $h,$ is decreasing at a constant rate of $6\\,\\text{in/h}.$ Find the rate at which the volume of sand is decreasing when the height of the sand is $9\\,\\text{in}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nHint: Assume that the rate of decrease is positive.\n\nThe sand volume is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{h}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's consider the dimensions of the tank. Let's call $H=24\\,\\text{in}$ the height of the conical tank, and $R=\\frac{16}{2}=8\\,\\text{in}$ its radius at the top base. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the sand level. We call $h$ the height of the sand pile and $r$ the radius of the top surface of the sand. Both of these quantities, and the sand volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-6\\,\\text{in}/\\text{h},\n$$\n\nand we want to know $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=9\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the tank and the remaining sand must be similar. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{8}{24} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{3}\\,h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{1}{3}h)}^{2}h \\\\ v & =\\frac{1}{27}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{27}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{9}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the rate at which the volume of sand is changing when $h=9\\,\\text{in}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{9}\\pi (9)^{2}(-6) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =-54\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sand volume is decreasing at a rate of $54\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{h}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523974 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064290 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93452be9-27a3-5c5d-b4f2-11b6b1896bca" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of Fluid Level Using Similar Solids"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69fb2899-b036-54ad-a324-ab7b25c590cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523473} {:answer/id #uuid "80795005-7359-5d8c-8f47-fa8cce93244e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523528} {:answer/id #uuid "8b4c38d6-9e86-5118-af92-02f7b458a6e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523542} {:answer/id #uuid "9e13c728-dd08-55eb-9f0d-ac9fa0d1a989" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523584} {:answer/id #uuid "b8f8255a-7a62-5923-8d18-5d266361f1cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523643}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b4c38d6-9e86-5118-af92-02f7b458a6e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523542} :answer-field/id #uuid "beb4e4f6-e48c-5e57-a5a4-043092d6909c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523885}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7d9fecf0-f483-51c1-b6ab-f1ee3ed71254" :question/math-academy-id "q-51396" :question/problem "![](images/86/8676d9a562d254a1194502c5a45368d5345f81aee3a066c28acc87cb1f33812c.png)\n\nThe height of an ice cream cone is $12\\,\\text{cm}$ and the radius at the top of the cone is $2\\,\\text{cm}.$ Ice cream is leaking from a small hole at the bottom at a rate of $2\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{min}.$ If the ice cream level is $6\\,\\text{cm}$ at a particular moment, how fast is the ice cream level decreasing at that moment?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's consider the dimensions of the cone. Let's call $H=12\\,\\text{cm}$ the height of the cone, and $R=2\\,\\text{cm}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the ice cream level. We call $h$ the height of the ice cream in the cone and $r$ the radius of the ice cream top surface. Both of these quantities, and the ice cream volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-2\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{min},\n$$\n\nand we want to find the rate of change of the ice cream level $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=6\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of the ice cream:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cone and the ice cream part of the cone must be similar. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{2}{12} \\\\ r & =\\frac{h}{6}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{h}{6})}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{108}\\pi {h}^{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{108}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{36}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of the ice cream level in the cone when $h=6\\,\\text{cm}.$ We can compute the value of $h$ at this moment as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\pi  & =\\frac{1}{36}\\pi ({6}^{2})\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-2\\,\\text{cm/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the height of the ice cream is decreasing at a rate of $2\\,\\text{cm/min}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064964 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064291 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ab44410-8968-52e0-983f-318416a68cb5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Rate of Change of Volume Given a Rate of Change of Radius or Height"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e3cb6f9-311e-5c6f-968f-d329ca876acf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "256\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/\\text{h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523349} {:answer/id #uuid "3d8c52e3-c501-5c7c-b667-05e94d257b23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "288\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/\\text{h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523384} {:answer/id #uuid "a8bb8f76-f47c-5af3-a659-05c32947975b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "128\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/\\text{h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523610} {:answer/id #uuid "d59b9e51-13c3-51f1-9ffd-54c886c3a3a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "396\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/\\text{h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523703} {:answer/id #uuid "e5e695d2-b727-526d-9159-909e8a375b4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "512\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/\\text{h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523737}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d8c52e3-c501-5c7c-b667-05e94d257b23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "288\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/\\text{h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523384} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b0f255b-23e8-5379-8b30-b0353a515cfe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "438beae6-a258-51c5-a564-575bb144d947" :question/math-academy-id "q-85823" :question/problem "The radius of the base of a cone is decreasing at a rate of $4\\,\\text{m}/\\text{h}.$ The height of the cone is always half its radius. At what rate is the cone's volume decreasing when the radius of its base is $12\\,\\text{m}?$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let's call $h$ the height of the cone and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-4\\,\\text{m}/\\text{h},\n$$\n\nand we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $r=12\\,\\text{m}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $r.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $h$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $h$ in terms of $r.$\n\nWe're told that the height of the cone is half its radius. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h & =\\frac{r}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{r}{2})=\\frac{1}{6}\\pi {r}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating just $v$ and $r,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{6}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi (12)^{2}(-4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =-288\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/\\text{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cone's volume is decreasing at a rate of $288\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/\\text{h}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524018 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064387 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b2d47c1-0f0c-5722-8778-6f2c3236401b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Rate of Change of Radius or Height Given a Rate of Change of Volume"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "056a5d91-b8d1-5034-8b2c-8042c880a87f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi \\,\\text{in}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523264} {:answer/id #uuid "7bf8fedf-c4d0-57a1-b87f-123455f19838" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\,\\text{in}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523512} {:answer/id #uuid "7c443a45-9404-54f4-93dd-6d71dc7f3bdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,\\text{in}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523514} {:answer/id #uuid "83cce91b-baa0-5b4c-a9d5-d79bc959c759" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2\\pi }\\,\\text{in}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523533} {:answer/id #uuid "b13b97bc-d673-581c-880d-84a73ebfe6a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\,\\text{in}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523627}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c443a45-9404-54f4-93dd-6d71dc7f3bdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,\\text{in}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523514} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a4767cd-3a6a-5b55-9fbe-f78b2bfcbb23" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d9e85e6a-5a02-52f7-ba3c-489084bc4a6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-85828" :question/problem "The volume of a cone is decreasing at a rate of $72\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{s}.$ The radius of the cone is always equal to its height. At what rate is the height of the cone decreasing when its height is $6\\,\\text{in}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's call $h$ the height of the cone and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-72\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{s},\n$$\n\nand we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=6\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nWe're told that the radius of the cone is equal to its height. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi (h)^{2}\\cdot h=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {h}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating just $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\pi {h}^{2}(\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-72 & =\\pi (6)^{2}(\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,\\text{in/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the height of the cone is decreasing at a rate of $\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,\\text{in}/\\text{s}.$"}]}
